<Page title="Work — Alexander Campos" description="Independent software, agent infrastructure, and a landscaping workflow prototype, informed by a background delivering systems in regulated finance.">
  <article class="work">
    <h1>Work</h1>
    <p class="lede">From a model response to something someone can use.</p>
    <p>
      I build AI products and the infrastructure around them: applications,
      memory, model routing, and workflows that turn output into a completed
      job. I’m interested in what becomes practical as intelligence gets cheaper,
      and what still stands between an answer and a result.
    </p>

    <section>
      <h2>Independent software</h2>
      <div class="build">
        <h3><a href="/projects/reframed">Reframed</a></h3>
        <p>
          A resume tool built around a simple concern: useful editing can quietly
          change what someone meant. It shows the changes so the applicant can
          decide what stays, and issues a signed record of the text comparison.
          I built the application, billing, storage, and model routing.
          The product is live. The next question is repeat use.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="build">
        <h3><a href="/projects/forgeframe">ForgeFrame</a></h3>
        <p>
          Open-source memory software for agents. It stores records locally,
          tracks their origin, and changes retrieval priority with use and time.
          I use it in my own tools and publish the code so other people can
          inspect it and run it themselves.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="build">
        <h3>Model routing and evaluation</h3>
        <p>
          I route requests across models and providers, with fallbacks when one
          fails. Picking a cheaper model only helps if it still completes the
          task: retries, review, and bad output belong in the cost calculation.
          I also do paid adversarial evaluation work in finance, designing cases
          and rubrics that distinguish plausible answers from defensible ones.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p><a href="/projects">The rest of the projects</a> include personal experiments and work I’ve parked.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="orellana">
      <h2>Orellana Landscaping</h2>
      <p>
        I built a quoting and invoicing prototype for a landscaping business
        in Woodbridge, Virginia. The problem was the work between a job in the
        field, a proposal, and a clean invoice in the books.
      </p>
      <p>
        The prototype turns field notes into a proposal and supports invoice
        creation, check-payment recording, and recurring invoice runs. There is
        a demo accounting backend and an adapter for QuickBooks. Spanish aliases
        help enter service names; the generated proposals are currently in English.
      </p>
      <h3>A small change to the proposal</h3>
      <p>
        I put payment options and a financing application link beside the price
        in the proposal. That information is useful while someone is deciding
        whether to take the job. Hiding it on an invoice means it arrives after
        the decision it could have helped.
      </p>
      <h3>What makes it usable</h3>
      <p>
        Accounting writes require a preview and confirmation. Invoice runs can
        resume after an interruption and skip items already sent. Those are
        implemented safeguards. Production use and an effect on sales remain
        unverified; this is a prototype case study.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Operations background</h2>
      <p>
        In bank operations, I own integration specifications and onboarding for
        more than 38 external vendors, from file formats and encryption through
        testing and production readiness. The work is making sure different
        organizations mean the same thing when they say a transmission succeeded.
      </p>
      <p>
        On an exchange handling more than 300 daily transmissions, I took anomaly
        detection from a prototype to an automated pipeline and two formally owned
        production controls. Manual monitoring fell 78%, leaving people to
        investigate exceptions instead of watching every transmission.
      </p>
      <p>
        When a failure affected more than 20,000 accounts, I wrote the SQL
        investigation, isolated the cause, and helped remediate it. We added
        monitoring at the failure points. That work taught me to look past a
        successful run: someone has to own the exceptions and be able to recover
        when the system fails.
      </p>
      <p class="note">Roles and dates are on <a href="/resume">my resume</a>.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What I’m paying attention to</h2>
      <p>
        My working thesis is that abundant intelligence moves the scarce part of
        a business. Prediction, software, and information become easier to produce.
        Turning them into consequential outcomes still depends on capital, physical
        infrastructure, data, permissions, distribution, and institutions.
      </p>
      <p>
        I want to build at those conversion points: find a useful capability,
        understand what keeps it from earning its keep, and build the missing
        piece. Who controls the capacity, captures the value, and carries the
        risk matters as much as what the model can do.
      </p>
      <p>
        I’m exploring those questions in
        <a href="/research/claims-capacity-collateral">Claims, capacity, and collateral</a>.
        The aim is to build things I can understand, own, and make dependable,
        while leaving the people who use them more capable.
      </p>
      <p>If that overlaps with something you’re working on, <a href="mailto:alex@campos.works">write to me</a>.</p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Page>

